# Source distribution contents — wattleflow-workflow (clean core).
#
# The wheel is built FROM this sdist, so what is pruned here stays out of the
# wheel as well. This file is therefore the packaging enforcement point for
# zero-trust (POLICY §7.1) — not just an sdist convenience.
#
# WHY IT IS AUTHORITATIVE: `global-exclude *` on the first directive line drops
# the default file list (including setuptools_scm's git-tracked set), so nothing
# ships unless it is explicitly re-added below. Delete that line and packaging
# silently reverts to "whatever git tracks" — the boundary stops being enforced.
#
# Ships: the import-clean package set — concrete, constants, decorators, enums,
#        helpers, managers, orchestrators, schedulers.
#
# Everything third-party-coupled — specialisations (connections/, drivers/,
# processors/, pipelines/, documents/, strategies/, blackboards/, mappers/,
# api/) and the third-party helpers — lives in the separate wattleflow
# distributions (wattleflow-processors, wattleflow-cad) or the examples project.
# The compliance layer (oscal/, decorators/oscal/, decorators/pspf.py) ships from
# wattleflow-processors too: it is a specialisation, not framework mechanics.
#
# "import-clean" = importable with only stdlib + the `wattleflow` core package.
# A third-party reference is allowed in the core ONLY as a *guarded optional
# dependency*: try/except with a functionally complete stdlib
# fallback. Installing this wheel never forces a third-party dependency.
# The YAML-backed configuration and its helpers moved to wattleflow-processors;
# what stays here is helpers/config.py — one module with one owner,
# never a namespace shared with another distribution.
#
# INVARIANT: this include list must cover every package that
# `[tool.setuptools.packages.find]` in pyproject.toml declares. A package
# declared there but absent here installs as an empty directory, so the
# consumer's `import` fails at runtime instead of at install time.

global-exclude *
global-exclude .*
global-exclude *.pyc
global-exclude *.json
global-exclude *.yaml
global-exclude __pycache__/*

# --- Clean core packages ---
# Every package `[tool.setuptools.packages.find]` declares must appear here;
# see the invariant in that file.
recursive-include src/wattleflow/concrete *.py
recursive-include src/wattleflow/constants *.py
recursive-include src/wattleflow/decorators *.py
recursive-include src/wattleflow/enums *.py
recursive-include src/wattleflow/helpers *.py
recursive-include src/wattleflow/managers *.py
recursive-include src/wattleflow/orchestrators *.py
recursive-include src/wattleflow/schedulers *.py

# --- Never ship the core (it ships via the separate `wattleflow` dependency) ---
# Defensive: core/ is absent from a clean checkout but reappears as a dev symlink,
# and must not be swept into this distribution when it does (mirrors the
# `wattleflow.core*` exclude in pyproject packages.find).
prune src/wattleflow/core

# --- Documentation lives in its own repository ---
# `documentation/` is a symlink to the separate wattleflow-documentation repo.
# Pruning keeps another repository's content — and its C-snapshots — out of this
# distribution (NFRQ-SEC-02, minimal surface). To ship the docs instead, drop the
# prune and write `recursive-include documentation *.md`: the directive takes a
# DIRECTORY plus a PATTERN, so a glob path such as `documentation/*.md` matches
# nothing and fails silently.
prune documentation
prune tests

# --- NFR enforcement tooling ---
# The controlled-vocabulary registry ships so the NFR lint is reproducible from
# the sdist; it is governed data, not an importable module. Both files
# are needed together — the lint carries no built-in vocabulary.
# (`global-exclude *.json` above means both registries need an explicit include.)
# tools/dictionary.json is the code vocabulary (the criterion wem_lint reads by
# default); the discourse vocabulary, documentation/dictionary.yaml, stays in the
# documentation repository and does not ship.
include tools/wem_core.py
include tools/wem_lint.py
include tools/dictionary.json
include tools/messages.json
include README.md LICENSE pyproject.toml requirements-dev.txt
